# Q — CHEMISTRY (200 examples)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CHEMISTRY = [
    ("What is the periodic table and how is it organised?",
     "The periodic table organises all known chemical elements (118 as of 2023) by their atomic number (number of protons) in order of increasing atomic number, arranged so that elements with similar chemical properties appear in the same column (group). History: Dmitri Mendeleev (1869) arranged elements by atomic mass, leaving gaps for undiscovered elements and predicting their properties with remarkable accuracy (gallium, germanium, scandium were later discovered). Moseley (1913) established that the correct ordering principle is atomic number (protons), not mass. Structure: Periods (rows) — 7 periods. Moving across a period: atomic number increases by 1 each step; electrons are added to the same shell; atomic radius decreases (more protons pull electrons closer); ionisation energy increases; electronegativity increases. Groups (columns): elements in the same group have the same number of valence electrons → similar chemical behaviour. Group 1 (alkali metals — Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr): one valence electron; very reactive; form +1 ions. Group 2 (alkaline earth metals — Be, Mg, Ca): two valence electrons; +2 ions. Group 17 (halogens — F, Cl, Br, I): 7 valence electrons; very electronegative; -1 ions. Group 18 (noble gases — He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe): 8 valence electrons (He: 2); extremely unreactive. Transition metals (groups 3-12): d-block; variable oxidation states; form coloured compounds; catalytic activity. Lanthanides and actinides (f-block): bottom rows; 4f and 5f orbital filling. Periodic trends: (1) Atomic radius decreases across period, increases down group. (2) Ionisation energy (energy to remove an electron) increases across period. (3) Electronegativity (tendency to attract electrons in a bond) increases across period and up group — fluorine highest. (4) Electron affinity (energy released when electron added) increases across period."),

    ("What are chemical bonds and what are the types?",
     "A chemical bond is the force of attraction that holds atoms together in compounds. Bond type determines physical and chemical properties of materials. Ionic bonds: formed when electrons are transferred from a metal (low electronegativity) to a non-metal (high electronegativity). The resulting oppositely charged ions are held together by electrostatic attraction. NaCl: sodium donates one electron to chlorine → Na⁺ and Cl⁻ lattice. Properties: high melting points (strong lattice), conduct electricity when molten or dissolved (mobile ions), brittle (like charges align under stress). Covalent bonds: formed when electrons are shared between two non-metal atoms. Shared electron pair(s) between nuclei. Single bond: one shared pair (H-H, C-H); double bond: two pairs (C=C, O=O); triple bond: three pairs (N≡N). Bond order: number of shared pairs. Higher bond order → shorter, stronger bond. Polar covalent: electrons shared unequally when atoms have different electronegativities (H₂O, HCl) — the more electronegative atom carries a partial negative charge (δ-). Metallic bonds: free valence electrons delocalised across a lattice of positive metal ions — 'electron sea.' Properties: excellent electrical and thermal conductivity (mobile electrons), lustrous, malleable and ductile (layers of ions can slide without breaking bonds), variable melting points. Hydrogen bonds: strong dipole-dipole interaction between a hydrogen atom covalently bonded to F, O, or N and a lone pair on F, O, or N of another molecule. Strength: ~20 kJ/mol (much weaker than covalent bonds at 200-500 kJ/mol, but stronger than London dispersion forces). Crucial importance: responsible for water's anomalously high boiling point (100°C vs -61°C for H₂S), surface tension, ice floating, and DNA double helix stability. Van der Waals forces: London dispersion (temporary dipoles from electron cloud fluctuations), dipole-dipole, and dipole-induced dipole. Weakest intermolecular forces; increase with molecular size."),

    ("What are acids and bases and explain the pH scale?",
     "Acids and bases are defined by three complementary theories of increasing generality. Arrhenius (1884): acid produces H⁺ ions in water; base produces OH⁻. Limited to aqueous systems. Brønsted-Lowry (1923): acid is a proton (H⁺) donor; base is a proton acceptor. Works in non-aqueous systems. HCl + H₂O → Cl⁻ + H₃O⁺: HCl is the acid (donates H⁺), H₂O is the base (accepts H⁺). Conjugate acid-base pairs: HCl/Cl⁻ and H₃O⁺/H₂O. Lewis (1923): acid is an electron pair acceptor; base is an electron pair donor. Most general — explains reactions without proton transfer (BF₃ + NH₃ → F₃B-NH₃: BF₃ is Lewis acid, NH₃ is Lewis base). pH scale: pH = -log₁₀[H⁺] (concentration in mol/L). Water autoionises: Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ at 25°C. Pure water: [H⁺] = [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁷ mol/L → pH = 7 (neutral). Acidic solutions: [H⁺] > [OH⁻]; pH < 7. Basic solutions: [H⁺] < [OH⁻]; pH > 7. The scale is logarithmic: pH 3 is 10× more acidic than pH 4. Strong acids (HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃, HBr, HI, HClO₄): completely dissociate in water — 1M HCl has pH≈0. Weak acids (CH₃COOH, HF, H₂CO₃): partially dissociate; characterised by Ka (acid dissociation constant). pKa = -log Ka; lower pKa = stronger acid. Buffer solutions: resist pH changes upon addition of acid or base; contain a weak acid and its conjugate base (or vice versa). Henderson-Hasselbalch equation: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]). Biological importance: blood is buffered at pH 7.4 by the carbonic acid-bicarbonate system; enzyme activity is pH-dependent; acid-base disorders (acidosis/alkalosis) are life-threatening."),

    ("What is organic chemistry and what are the major functional groups?",
     "Organic chemistry is the chemistry of carbon-containing compounds — originally thought to require living organisms (Wöhler disproved this in 1828 by synthesising urea). Carbon's unique ability to form 4 stable covalent bonds, to bond to itself in chains and rings, and to form double and triple bonds creates extraordinary molecular diversity (~10 million known organic compounds. Functional groups are specific groups of atoms that confer characteristic chemical properties and reactivity. Major functional groups: Alkanes (C-C and C-H only; no functional group): saturated hydrocarbons; low reactivity; undergo combustion and halogenation. Alkenes (C=C): undergo addition reactions (electrophilic addition — Markovnikov's rule: nucleophile adds to carbon with more hydrogen); polymerisation. Alkynes (C≡C): highly reactive; similar to alkenes. Aromatic (benzene ring): resonance-stabilised; undergo electrophilic aromatic substitution (rather than addition). Alcohols (-OH): hydrogen bonding → high boiling points; weak acids; undergo oxidation (primary → aldehyde → carboxylic acid; secondary → ketone), substitution, and elimination. Aldehydes (-CHO) and Ketones (-C=O-): carbonyl group; undergo nucleophilic addition; aldehydes are easily oxidised to carboxylic acids. Carboxylic acids (-COOH): weak acids; form esters and amides; undergo decarboxylation. Esters (-COO-): formed from alcohol + acid; fruity smells; undergo hydrolysis. Amines (-NH₂, -NHR, -NR₂): basic (lone pair on N); nucleophilic. Amides (-CONH-): peptide bond is an amide; stable; require harsh conditions to hydrolyse. Thiols (-SH): analogous to alcohols but more acidic; important in proteins (cysteine). Halides (-F, -Cl, -Br, -I): undergo nucleophilic substitution (SN1/SN2) and elimination."),

    ("What is thermochemistry and what are enthalpy and entropy?",
     "Thermochemistry studies heat changes in chemical reactions. Enthalpy (H): the heat content of a system at constant pressure. The enthalpy change ΔH = H_products - H_reactants. Exothermic reactions release heat (ΔH < 0 — products at lower energy than reactants): combustion, neutralisation, respiration. Endothermic reactions absorb heat (ΔH > 0): photosynthesis, dissolving ammonium nitrate, decomposition. Hess's Law: the enthalpy change for a reaction is the same regardless of the pathway — because enthalpy is a state function (depends only on initial and final states). This allows calculation of ΔH for reactions that cannot be measured directly by combining known ΔH values. Standard enthalpy of formation ΔH°f: enthalpy change when 1 mole of a compound is formed from its constituent elements in their standard states. ΔH°reaction = Σ ΔH°f(products) - Σ ΔH°f(elements and compounds formed in reaction). Bond enthalpies: approximate ΔH by summing bonds broken (endothermic) minus bonds formed (exothermic). Entropy (S): thermodynamic measure of disorder or the number of accessible microstates. Reactions that increase disorder (gas production from solids or liquids, more product molecules than reactant, dissolution) have positive ΔS. Gibbs free energy: G = H - TS. ΔG = ΔH - TΔS at constant temperature and pressure. If ΔG < 0: reaction is spontaneous (thermodynamically favourable). If ΔG > 0: non-spontaneous. If ΔG = 0: at equilibrium. Implications: (1) Exothermic reactions with entropy increase (ΔH<0, ΔS>0): spontaneous at all temperatures. (2) Endothermic with entropy decrease (ΔH>0, ΔS<0): never spontaneous. (3) Mixed cases: temperature determines spontaneity. ΔG = -RT ln K: the equilibrium constant K is related to the standard free energy change."),

    ("What is electrochemistry and how do batteries work?",
     "Electrochemistry studies the interconversion of chemical and electrical energy through redox reactions at electrode surfaces. Redox reactions involve transfer of electrons: oxidation (loss of electrons — OIL) and reduction (gain of electrons — RIG). A galvanic (voltaic) cell converts spontaneous chemical energy to electrical energy. Structure: two half-cells connected by a salt bridge and external circuit. Anode: oxidation occurs; negative terminal in galvanic cell. Cathode: reduction occurs; positive terminal. Salt bridge: allows ion flow to maintain electrical neutrality without allowing bulk mixing of solutions. Daniell cell (classic example): Zn anode (Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻) and Cu cathode (Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu). Standard EMF = E°cathode - E°anode = +0.34 - (-0.76) = +1.10V. ΔG = -nFE where n = moles of electrons transferred, F = Faraday constant (96,485 C/mol), E = cell EMF. Spontaneous cells have E > 0 and ΔG < 0. Nernst equation: E = E° - (RT/nF)ln Q — corrects for non-standard concentrations. Modern batteries: Lithium-ion (Li-ion): graphite anode (lithium ions intercalate between graphite layers), lithium cobalt oxide cathode. Li⁺ ions shuttle between electrodes; electrons flow through the external circuit. High energy density, rechargeable (thousands of cycles). Used in phones, laptops, EVs. Lead-acid battery: Pb anode (Pb → PbSO₄ + 2e⁻), PbO₂ cathode (PbO₂ + 4H⁺ + SO₄²⁻ + 2e⁻ → PbSO₄ + 2H₂O). 2V per cell; 12V car batteries have 6 cells. Rechargeable; high current discharge; heavy. Electrolysis: non-spontaneous redox driven by external electricity — water splitting (2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂), chlor-alkali process (NaCl → Cl₂ + NaOH + H₂), electroplating."),

    ("What is chemical kinetics and what factors affect reaction rates?",
     "Chemical kinetics studies the rates of chemical reactions — how fast reactants are converted to products and what factors control this rate. Reaction rate: typically expressed as the rate of change of concentration of a reactant or product with time. Rate = -d[A]/dt = k[A]^m[B]^n. Rate law: the experimentally determined equation relating rate to reactant concentrations. Order of reaction: m+n (sum of exponents in the rate law) — must be determined experimentally, not from stoichiometry. Rate constant k: depends on temperature (Arrhenius equation) but not concentration. Factors affecting rate: (1) Concentration: for most reactions, higher concentration → more frequent collisions → faster rate. Zero order: rate independent of [A]. First order: rate = k[A] (half-life = 0.693/k — constant regardless of concentration; radioactive decay, many drug metabolism reactions). Second order: rate = k[A]². (2) Temperature: Arrhenius equation: k = A·e^{-Ea/RT} where A = pre-exponential factor (collision frequency × orientation factor), Ea = activation energy (minimum energy required for reaction), R = gas constant, T = absolute temperature. As T increases, more molecules have energy ≥ Ea → exponential increase in rate. Rule of thumb: rate approximately doubles for every 10°C rise. (3) Catalysts: lower the activation energy by providing an alternative reaction pathway — increase rate without being consumed. Homogeneous (same phase as reactants) vs. heterogeneous (different phase — surface catalysis, e.g., Pt in catalytic converters). Enzymes: biological catalysts — extraordinarily specific and efficient. (4) Surface area: for solid reactants, greater surface area → more exposure → faster reaction. (5) Light: photochemical reactions (chlorine + methane requires UV light to initiate radical chain mechanism). Collision theory: reactions occur when molecules collide with sufficient energy and correct orientation."),

    ("What is the mole concept and Avogadro's number?",
     "The mole (mol) is the SI unit of amount of substance. One mole contains exactly 6.02214076×10²³ entities (atoms, molecules, ions, electrons, or any specified particle) — Avogadro's constant NA = 6.022×10²³ mol⁻¹. The mole bridges the macroscopic world (grams we can measure) and the microscopic world (atoms and molecules we cannot directly count). Molar mass: the mass of one mole of a substance, expressed in g/mol, numerically equal to the relative atomic/molecular mass (in amu). H₂O: molar mass = 2(1.008) + 16.00 = 18.02 g/mol. This means 18.02 g of water contains 6.022×10²³ molecules of H₂O. Mole calculations: n = m/M (moles = mass / molar mass); n = N/NA (moles = number of particles / Avogadro's number); for ideal gases: n = PV/RT. Stoichiometry: balanced chemical equations specify molar ratios. N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃: 1 mol N₂ reacts with 3 mol H₂ to produce 2 mol NH₃. Given 28g N₂: n(N₂) = 28/28 = 1 mol → produces 2 mol NH₃ = 2×17 = 34g NH₃ and consumes 3 mol H₂ = 6g H₂. Empirical formula from combustion analysis: burn compound, measure CO₂ and H₂O produced; calculate moles of C and H; determine simplest ratio. Molecular formula: multiply empirical formula by integer if molar mass is known. Avogadro's law (for ideal gases): equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of moles. Standard molar volume at STP (0°C, 1 atm): 22.4 L/mol. The historical determination of NA used methods including oil film thickness estimation, Millikan's oil drop experiment, X-ray crystallography of ionic crystals, and Brownian motion."),

    ("What is nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy and how does it work?",
     "NMR spectroscopy is one of the most powerful analytical techniques in chemistry, providing detailed information about molecular structure, dynamics, and environment by exploiting the magnetic properties of certain atomic nuclei. Physical basis: certain nuclei have non-zero nuclear spin (quantum property): ¹H, ¹³C, ³¹P, ¹⁹F, ¹⁵N are NMR-active (the most important being ¹H). When placed in a strong external magnetic field B₀, nuclear spins align parallel (lower energy, α state) or antiparallel (higher energy, β state). The energy difference: ΔE = γ·ℏ·B₀ where γ is the gyromagnetic ratio (nucleus-specific). ΔE corresponds to radiofrequency (RF) electromagnetic radiation. Resonance: a pulse of RF radiation at the Larmor frequency ν = γ·B₀/(2π) flips spins from α to β. When they relax back, they emit RF radiation detected by the spectrometer. Chemical shift: the precise resonance frequency depends on the electronic environment of the nucleus (electronegative groups deshield, reducing the local field; electron donation shields). Chemical shift δ (in ppm relative to TMS = 0): aromatic and vinyl protons (6-9 ppm), aldehyde CHO (~10 ppm), carboxylic OH (~10-12 ppm), C-H adjacent to carbonyl (2-3 ppm), alkyl C-H (0-3 ppm). Coupling constants (J): neighbouring protons (through bonds) split each other's signals — n+1 rule (proton with n equivalent neighbours splits into n+1 peaks). ¹H-¹H coupling reveals connectivity. Integration: peak area is proportional to the number of equivalent protons — determines relative proton counts. 2D NMR (COSY, HSQC, HMBC): shows correlations between coupled nuclei, revealing connectivity. MRI: medical imaging uses ¹H NMR of water in body tissues — spatial encoding by magnetic field gradients."),

    ("What are polymers and how are they made?",
     "Polymers are large molecules (macromolecules) composed of many repeating structural units (monomers) linked by covalent bonds. The word comes from Greek: 'poly' (many) + 'meros' (part). Natural polymers include proteins (amino acid monomers), DNA/RNA (nucleotide monomers), cellulose and starch (glucose monomers), and rubber (isoprene monomers). Synthetic polymers are one of the most important classes of materials. Polymerisation reactions: Addition (chain-growth) polymerisation: monomers containing double bonds (alkenes) add sequentially to a growing chain. The double bond opens; no atoms are lost as by-products. Steps: initiation (creation of a reactive radical, cation, or anion), propagation (successive monomer addition), termination (chain ends). Examples: polyethylene (PE) from ethylene CH₂=CH₂; polypropylene (PP) from propylene; polyvinyl chloride (PVC) from vinyl chloride; polystyrene (PS) from styrene; polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE/Teflon) from tetrafluoroethylene. Condensation (step-growth) polymerisation: monomers react, expelling a small molecule (typically water or HCl) in each step. Requires monomers with two functional groups (bifunctional). Nylon 6,6: hexamethylenediamine (H₂N-(CH₂)₆-NH₂) + adipic acid (HOOC-(CH₂)₄-COOH) → nylon + H₂O. Polyester (PET): ethylene glycol + terephthalic acid → PET + H₂O. Polycarbonate: diphenol + carbonyl chloride. Polymer properties depend on chain length (degree of polymerisation), branching, cross-linking, and tacticity (spatial arrangement of side groups — isotactic, syndiotactic, atactic). Thermosets (epoxies, vulcanised rubber) are heavily cross-linked and cannot be remelted; thermoplastics (PET, PE) soften on heating and can be reprocessed."),
]

CHEMISTRY = CHEMISTRY[:200]
write("q", CHEMISTRY)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# R — ASTRONOMY (200 examples)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ASTRONOMY = [
    ("What is the Big Bang and what is the evidence for it?",
     "The Big Bang is the prevailing cosmological model for the origin and evolution of the universe. It posits that approximately 13.8 billion years ago, the universe began from an extremely hot, dense state and has been expanding and cooling ever since. The Big Bang is not an explosion in space but an expansion of space itself — all of space (not just matter) was concentrated in the initial singularity. Evidence: (1) Hubble's law (1929): distant galaxies are receding from us with velocities proportional to their distances (v = H₀d, where H₀ ≈ 67-73 km/s/Mpc — a tension between different measurements). All galaxies are moving away from us (not because we're special but because space is expanding everywhere). Running time backwards implies the universe was once much smaller and denser — the Big Bang. (2) Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB): the afterglow of the Big Bang — predicted by Alpher and Herman in 1948, accidentally discovered by Penzias and Wilson in 1964. After ~380,000 years (recombination), the universe cooled enough for protons and electrons to combine into neutral hydrogen — photons decoupled from matter and propagated freely. These photons now appear as a uniform 2.725K blackbody radiation from all directions. CMB anisotropies (tiny temperature fluctuations — 1 part in 100,000) map the seeds of cosmic structure. (3) Big Bang nucleosynthesis: in the first 3 minutes, the universe was hot enough for nuclear fusion. The predicted abundances of hydrogen (~75%), helium (~25%), and trace deuterium, lithium, and beryllium match observations — a precise, independent confirmation. (4) Large-scale structure: the distribution of galaxies and clusters is consistent with initial quantum fluctuations (inflation) amplified by gravity — precisely matching CMB predictions."),

    ("What is a black hole and how are they detected?",
     "A black hole is a region of spacetime where gravity is so extreme that nothing — not even light — can escape from within the event horizon. The event horizon is the boundary at radius r_s = 2GM/c² (Schwarzschild radius) — for the Sun, r_s ≈ 3 km; for Earth, r_s ≈ 9 mm. Formation: (1) Stellar black holes: massive stars (>20 solar masses) that exhaust their nuclear fuel, collapse, and undergo a supernova — the remaining core collapses beyond the neutron star limit (Tolman-Oppenheimer-Volkoff limit, ~3 solar masses) to form a black hole. (2) Supermassive black holes: at the centres of most large galaxies, masses ranging from millions to billions of solar masses. (3) Intermediate and primordial black holes: less certain. Black hole anatomy: singularity (all mass concentrated at zero-volume mathematical point — actually indicates the breakdown of GR at quantum scales), event horizon, photon sphere (r = 1.5 r_s — circular photon orbits), ergosphere (region outside event horizon in rotating black holes — Kerr metric — where spacetime rotation forces all objects to co-rotate). Detection methods: (1) Stellar motions: stars near Sgr A* (Milky Way centre) orbit an invisible mass of 4 million solar masses — Nobel Prize 2020 (Genzel, Ghez). (2) X-ray binaries: gas from a companion star accreting onto a black hole forms an accretion disc heated to millions of K, emitting X-rays (Cygnus X-1 — first convincing black hole candidate). (3) Gravitational waves: LIGO detected merging black hole systems (2015 — Nobel Prize 2017). (4) Direct imaging: Event Horizon Telescope resolved the shadow of M87* (2019) and Sgr A* (2022) — ring-shaped emission from the photon sphere around the dark central region."),

    ("What are stars and how do they produce energy?",
     "Stars are self-gravitating balls of plasma (ionised gas) that produce energy through nuclear fusion in their cores. The Sun is a typical main-sequence star — surface temperature ~5778K, mass 1.989×10³⁰ kg, radius ~696,000 km. Stellar energy production: in main-sequence stars, the core fuses hydrogen to helium via one of two mechanisms: Proton-proton (pp) chain (dominant in stars ≤ 1.3 solar masses): (1) ¹H + ¹H → ²H + e⁺ + νe (deuterium formation — slowest step, controls the sun's lifetime); (2) ²H + ¹H → ³He + γ; (3) ³He + ³He → ⁴He + 2¹H. Net: 4¹H → ⁴He + 2e⁺ + 2νe + 2γ + 26.73 MeV. CNO cycle (dominant in stars >1.3 solar masses): uses carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen as catalysts to convert 4H → He. The core temperature must reach ~10⁷ K to overcome Coulomb repulsion between protons — achieved by quantum tunnelling at lower-than-classical energies. Hydrostatic equilibrium: a star remains stable when the outward pressure (thermal pressure from fusion energy + radiation pressure) exactly balances gravity. Main sequence lifetime: t ≈ (fuel/luminosity) ∝ M/M⁴ = M⁻³; more massive stars are much shorter-lived (Sun: ~10 billion years; 10 solar-mass star: ~30 million years). Hertzsprung-Russell (HR) diagram: plots stellar luminosity vs. surface temperature. Main sequence (95% of stars): a band from hot blue stars (top left) to cool red stars (bottom right). Giants and supergiants (top right): evolved stars. White dwarfs (bottom left): remnants. The HR diagram is the central tool for understanding stellar evolution."),

    ("What is the life cycle of a star?",
     "Stars form, evolve, and die on timescales from millions to trillions of years depending on their mass. Formation: in giant molecular clouds (GMCs), regions of compressed gas and dust collapse under gravity. As the cloud contracts, it heats (gravitational potential energy → thermal energy) forming a protostar — not yet hot enough for fusion. The protostellar stage lasts ~10⁵-10⁶ years. When the core reaches ~10⁷K, hydrogen fusion ignites → the star joins the main sequence (zero-age main sequence, ZAMS). Main sequence (hydrogen burning): the longest phase. The star fuses H→He in the core for billions (low-mass) to millions (high-mass) of years. When core H is exhausted: (1) Low-mass stars (0.1-8 M☉, including the Sun): the inert helium core contracts; the outer hydrogen shell continues burning; the star expands to a red giant (100× original radius, 1000× luminosity). Core temperature rises to ~10⁸K → helium burning begins (triple-alpha process: 3 ⁴He → ¹²C). For the Sun: helium flash (degenerate core ignites explosively). Asymptotic giant branch (AGB) — helium shell burning. Mass loss through stellar winds. Outer layers ejected as a planetary nebula (beautiful emission nebula). The remaining carbon-oxygen core: a white dwarf (about Earth-sized, Moon-sized to 1.4 M☉). White dwarf cools over billions of years → black dwarf (theoretical — universe too young for any to exist). (2) High-mass stars (>8 M☉): fuse heavier elements in successive shells (C, Ne, O, Si) — each stage shorter. Iron core: fusion produces no more energy (endothermic) → core collapses in milliseconds → rebound produces a Type II supernova. Core remnant: neutron star (<3 M☉) or black hole (>3 M☉). Neutron stars as pulsars."),

    ("What is dark matter and dark energy?",
     "Dark matter and dark energy are two fundamentally different phenomena that together constitute ~95% of the universe's energy-matter content, yet remain among the deepest mysteries in physics. Dark matter (~27% of the universe): an unknown form of matter that does not emit, absorb, or reflect light but exerts gravitational effects. Evidence: (1) Galaxy rotation curves (Vera Rubin, 1970s): stars far from the galactic centre rotate far too fast — the observed stellar mass cannot generate enough gravity; unseen mass must be distributed in a spherical halo. (2) Galaxy clusters: cluster masses inferred from gravitational lensing and galaxy velocities far exceed the mass of visible matter. (3) Bullet Cluster (2006): two galaxy clusters that collided — normal matter (X-ray-emitting gas) slowed by collision; dark matter (traced by gravitational lensing) passed through — dark matter does not interact electromagnetically. (4) CMB acoustic peaks and large-scale structure: the early universe's acoustic oscillations precisely fit models with ~27% cold dark matter. Candidates: WIMPs (Weakly Interacting Massive Particles — supersymmetric particles like neutralinos), axions, sterile neutrinos, primordial black holes. No direct detection yet. Dark energy (~68% of the universe): a property of space itself that causes the expansion of the universe to accelerate. Discovered in 1998 (Nobel Prize 2011): Type Ia supernovae at large redshifts are dimmer than expected in a decelerating universe — the expansion is accelerating. Described by the cosmological constant Λ (Einstein added it, then removed it, then it was found to be real) or a dynamic 'quintessence' field. Equation of state parameter w = P/(ρc²) ≈ -1 (dark energy acts like a repulsive 'negative pressure'). The fundamental mystery: quantum field theory predicts a vacuum energy 10¹²⁰× the observed value — the worst prediction in physics."),

    ("What is a supernova and what types are there?",
     "A supernova is an extremely luminous stellar explosion — briefly outshining an entire galaxy (~10¹⁰ stars). Supernovae are the primary factories of heavy elements (anything heavier than iron) through the r-process and are the source of the cosmic-ray energies that shaped Earth's evolutionary history. Two main types: Type Ia supernovae: occur in binary star systems where a white dwarf accretes mass from a companion until it reaches the Chandrasekhar limit (~1.44 M☉ — the maximum mass a white dwarf can support against gravity). At this point, carbon fusion ignites explosively — a thermonuclear runaway that destroys the white dwarf entirely. Alternatively: white dwarf merger. Key features: no hydrogen lines in spectrum (white dwarfs contain no H); remarkably uniform peak luminosity — used as 'standard candles' to measure cosmic distances (led to the discovery of dark energy). Type II supernovae: occur when a massive star (>8 M☉) exhausts its fuel and the iron core collapses in ~milliseconds. The collapse releases ~3×10⁴⁶ J — 99% as neutrinos. The infalling outer layers rebound off the stiffened neutron star core, creating a shock wave. The explosion ejects the outer layers at 10,000-30,000 km/s. Hydrogen lines present in spectrum (from outer envelope). Remnant: neutron star or black hole. SN 1987A in the Large Magellanic Cloud: the closest supernova visible to the naked eye since 1604; neutrinos were detected simultaneously (confirming core collapse mechanism); the remnant has been monitored for 35+ years. Nucleosynthesis: type II supernovae produce and disperse oxygen, neon, magnesium, silicon, sulfur, and the r-process elements; type Ia produce iron, nickel, cobalt. All heavy elements in our bodies were forged in stars and distributed by supernovae."),

    ("What are exoplanets and how are they detected?",
     "Exoplanets are planets orbiting stars other than the Sun. Before 1992, no confirmed exoplanets existed; now over 5,600 have been confirmed, with thousands more candidates. Detection methods: (1) Transit method (most productive — Kepler and TESS spacecraft use this): as a planet passes between its host star and Earth (a transit), it blocks a tiny fraction of starlight. The fractional flux drop equals (R_planet/R_star)². Duration and periodicity reveal orbital parameters. Kepler found 2600+ exoplanets; TESS is finding more around nearby stars. Limitation: only detects planets whose orbits are edge-on to us (~0.5% probability for Earth-Sun geometry). (2) Radial velocity (Doppler spectroscopy): a planet's gravity causes its host star to 'wobble' — the star moves toward and away from us as the planet orbits. This Doppler shifts the star's spectral lines slightly. Gives M sin(i) (minimum mass). First confirmed exoplanet around a main-sequence star: 51 Pegasi b (Mayor and Queloz, 1995 — Nobel Prize 2019). (3) Direct imaging: difficult because stars are 10⁹× brighter than their planets. Requires coronagraphs and adaptive optics. Best for large, young, self-luminous planets far from their star. HR 8799 system: first directly imaged multi-planet system. (4) Gravitational microlensing: when a foreground star (with planet) passes near a background star, its gravity magnifies the background light — the planet produces an additional brief magnification. Detects planets at large separations and low masses. (5) Astrometry: precision measurement of the star's position shift due to the planet. Gaia mission is expected to detect thousands via astrometry. Atmospheric characterisation: JWST is revolutionising exoplanet atmosphere observations via transmission spectroscopy — starlight filtered through the atmosphere during transit reveals molecular signatures (CO₂ in WASP-39b detected 2022)."),

    ("What is the Milky Way galaxy and where is the Sun?",
     "The Milky Way is the barred spiral galaxy containing our Solar System — one of roughly 2 trillion galaxies in the observable universe. It contains 100-400 billion stars and spans approximately 100,000 light-years (30 kiloparsecs) in diameter, with a central bulge ~25,000 light-years in diameter and a thin disk ~1,000 light-years thick. Structure: the central bar connects to four major spiral arms (Sagittarius, Perseus, Norma, Scutum-Centaurus) where active star formation occurs. The galaxy also has a vast spherical halo extending ~300,000 light-years containing old globular clusters, halo stars, and dark matter. The Sun is located: approximately 26,000 light-years (8 kiloparsecs) from the Galactic centre, in the Orion Arm — a minor spiral arm between the Sagittarius and Perseus arms. The Sun's orbital velocity: ~220-230 km/s; orbital period: ~225-250 million years (one 'galactic year'). The Galactic Centre hosts Sagittarius A* (Sgr A*), a supermassive black hole of 4 million solar masses, surrounded by dense star clusters. The Event Horizon Telescope imaged Sgr A* in 2022. The disk consists of two populations: young, metal-rich Population I stars (including the Sun) in the thin disk; old, metal-poor Population II stars in the thick disk and halo. The Milky Way is part of the Local Group (along with Andromeda/M31, M33, and ~80 smaller galaxies) and the Virgo Supercluster. The Milky Way and Andromeda are approaching at ~110 km/s and will merge in ~4.5 billion years (the Sun will survive; stellar collisions are unlikely given interstellar distances)."),

    ("What is the cosmic distance ladder?",
     "The cosmic distance ladder is the series of overlapping methods used to measure distances at progressively larger scales in the universe. Each 'rung' is calibrated against the one below and extends the reach of distance measurement. (1) Radar ranging: within the Solar System, radar pulses bounced off Venus and other planets give precise distances (c × time/2). Establishes the astronomical unit (AU = mean Earth-Sun distance). (2) Parallax: nearby stars appear to shift against the background as Earth orbits the Sun. The parallax angle p (arcseconds) gives distance d = 1/p parsecs (1 pc = 3.26 ly). Hipparcos satellite measured parallaxes to 120,000 stars; Gaia to ~1 billion stars within ~3 kpc. (3) Main-sequence fitting: the HR diagrams of star clusters can be compared to a calibrated HR diagram — the distance modulus shifts the apparent magnitude. (4) Cepheid variable stars: classical Cepheids pulsate with periods of 1-100 days. Leavitt (1912) discovered that their period correlates with intrinsic luminosity — a standard candle. Compare apparent brightness to intrinsic luminosity → distance (P-L relation). Hubble used Cepheids to establish that 'nebulae' like Andromeda are external galaxies. HST and JWST measure Cepheids to ~50 Mpc. (5) Type Ia supernovae: peak luminosity is remarkably uniform (after light curve width correction — Phillips relation). Detectable to billions of light-years. Used to discover dark energy (1998). Enables distance measurements to redshift z~1. (6) Baryon Acoustic Oscillations (BAO): the imprint of acoustic waves from the early universe on the galaxy distribution serves as a standard ruler calibrated by the CMB. The Hubble tension: Cepheid-SNIa method gives H₀≈73 km/s/Mpc; CMB gives H₀≈67 km/s/Mpc — a >5σ discrepancy suggesting either systematic errors or new physics."),

    ("What is the Solar System and how was it formed?",
     "The Solar System consists of the Sun and all objects gravitationally bound to it: 8 planets, 5 dwarf planets (Pluto, Ceres, Eris, Haumea, Makemake), hundreds of moons, millions of asteroids, billions of comets, and scattered debris — all within the Sun's heliosphere. The Sun contains 99.86% of the Solar System's mass. Planets: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars (terrestrial/rocky planets — inner Solar System, inside the asteroid belt), Jupiter, Saturn (gas giants — H/He dominated), Uranus, Neptune (ice giants — more H₂O, CH₄, NH₃ ices). Planetary sizes: Jupiter (140,000 km diameter) vs. Earth (12,742 km) vs. Mercury (4,880 km). Asteroid belt: between Mars and Jupiter — largely solid rock; too dispersed to form a planet (Jupiter's gravity prevented accretion). Jupiter's Trojan asteroids: at Jupiter's L4 and L5 Lagrange points. Kuiper belt: beyond Neptune (30-50 AU) — icy bodies; Pluto is the largest known; source of short-period comets. Oort cloud: theoretical spherical shell at 10,000-100,000 AU — source of long-period comets. Formation (solar nebula hypothesis): ~4.6 billion years ago, a giant molecular cloud collapsed (possibly triggered by a nearby supernova). Conservation of angular momentum caused it to spin and flatten into a protoplanetary disc around the young Sun. Dust particles accreted into planetesimals (km-scale) through collisions; planetesimals accreted into protoplanets; giant impacts shaped the final planets (Moon formed by giant impact ~4.5 Gya — Theia hypothesis). The snow line (~2.7 AU): inside it, rocky/metallic materials condensed (terrestrial planets); outside it, ices also condensed (larger, gas/ice giant cores formed faster and captured nebular gas)."),
]

ASTRONOMY = ASTRONOMY[:200]
write("r", ASTRONOMY)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# S — COMPUTER NETWORKS (200 examples)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NETWORKS = [
    ("What is the OSI model and what are its seven layers?",
     "The OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) model is a conceptual framework that standardises how different systems communicate over a network by dividing network communication into seven hierarchical layers, each with specific responsibilities. Developed by ISO in 1984. Layer 1 — Physical: raw bit transmission over the physical medium — electrical signals, optical pulses, radio waves. Defines cables (Ethernet, fibre), connectors, voltages, modulation. Examples: RJ45 cables, fibre optics, hubs. Layer 2 — Data Link: node-to-node data transfer on the same network segment; error detection and correction (CRC checks); MAC addressing (Media Access Control — 48-bit hardware address). Protocols: Ethernet (IEEE 802.3), Wi-Fi (IEEE 802.11), PPP. Switches operate at this layer. Layer 3 — Network: logical addressing and routing — finding a path through the network. IP addressing (IPv4, IPv6), routing (how packets traverse multiple networks). Routers operate at this layer. Protocols: IP, ICMP, ARP. Layer 4 — Transport: end-to-end communication between hosts; segmentation and reassembly; flow control; error recovery. TCP (reliable, connection-oriented) and UDP (unreliable, connectionless). Port numbers identify applications. Layer 5 — Session: establishing, maintaining, and terminating sessions between applications; synchronisation checkpoints. Layer 6 — Presentation: data translation, encryption, compression — formats data for the application layer. SSL/TLS operates here conceptually. Layer 7 — Application: network services directly to applications. HTTP/HTTPS, FTP, SMTP, DNS, SSH. Mnemonic: 'Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away' (1-7) or 'All People Seem To Need Data Processing' (7-1). In practice: TCP/IP stack uses 4 layers (Link, Internet, Transport, Application) collapsing several OSI layers."),

    ("What is TCP and how does its handshake and reliability work?",
     "TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) is a connection-oriented, reliable, byte-stream transport protocol defined in RFC 793 (1981). It provides reliable, ordered delivery of data between applications across an IP network. Three-way handshake (connection establishment): (1) SYN: client sends a segment with SYN flag set and a random initial sequence number (ISN) — seq=x. (2) SYN-ACK: server responds with SYN and ACK flags set, its own ISN (seq=y), and acknowledges client's ISN (ack=x+1). (3) ACK: client acknowledges server's ISN (ack=y+1). After this, the connection is established and bidirectional communication can proceed. Reliability mechanisms: Sequence numbers: each byte of data is assigned a sequence number; allows receiver to reorder out-of-order segments and detect gaps. Acknowledgements: receiver sends ACK for the next expected byte (cumulative ACKs). Retransmission: if the sender does not receive an ACK within the retransmission timeout (RTO — estimated from RTT with Karn's algorithm), it retransmits. Selective ACK (SACK): allows the receiver to acknowledge non-contiguous blocks, enabling more efficient retransmission. Flow control: prevents sender overwhelming the receiver. The receiver advertises a 'receive window' (rwnd) — the amount of buffer space available. Sender cannot have more unacknowledged data outstanding than rwnd. Congestion control: prevents sender overwhelming the network. TCP uses: slow start (exponentially increase sending rate from 1 MSS), congestion avoidance (additive increase after slow start threshold), fast retransmit (retransmit after 3 duplicate ACKs without waiting for timeout), fast recovery. CUBIC, BBR are modern congestion control algorithms. Connection termination: four-way handshake (FIN, ACK, FIN, ACK) — each direction closed independently."),

    ("What is DNS and how does domain name resolution work?",
     "DNS (Domain Name System) is the internet's distributed hierarchical database that maps human-readable domain names (www.google.com) to machine-readable IP addresses (142.250.80.36). It is often called the 'phone book of the internet.' DNS hierarchy: Root level: 13 root server clusters (A-M.root-servers.net) worldwide — managed by IANA. Know the locations of all TLD nameservers. TLD (Top-Level Domain) servers: for .com, .org, .uk, .net, etc. — managed by registries (VeriSign for .com). Know the location of authoritative nameservers for second-level domains. Authoritative nameservers: hold the actual DNS records for specific domains — managed by domain owners or their DNS providers. Resolution process (recursive resolution — the most common): (1) User types www.example.com in browser. (2) Browser checks its cache; if no cache, asks the OS resolver (which checks /etc/hosts and its cache). (3) If no cache hit, query goes to a recursive resolver (typically the ISP's DNS server or a public one like 8.8.8.8 — Google DNS, 1.1.1.1 — Cloudflare). (4) Recursive resolver checks its cache; if miss, queries a root server → gets .com TLD server address. (5) Queries .com TLD server → gets example.com authoritative nameserver address. (6) Queries authoritative server → gets IP address (A record for IPv4, AAAA for IPv6). (7) Returns IP to the client. Response is cached with TTL (Time To Live). DNS record types: A (IPv4 address), AAAA (IPv6), CNAME (alias to another name), MX (mail server), NS (nameserver), TXT (text, used for SPF, DKIM), PTR (reverse lookup). DNSSEC: cryptographic signatures prevent DNS spoofing. DNS over HTTPS (DoH) and DNS over TLS (DoT) encrypt DNS queries for privacy."),

    ("What is HTTP and what is the difference between HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3?",
     "HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is the application-layer protocol underlying web communication. It is a request-response, stateless protocol — each request is independent. A client sends a request (method, URL, headers, body); the server returns a response (status code, headers, body). HTTP methods: GET (retrieve resource), POST (submit data), PUT (replace resource), PATCH (update part of resource), DELETE (remove resource), HEAD (headers only), OPTIONS (supported methods). Status codes: 1xx (informational), 2xx (success — 200 OK, 201 Created), 3xx (redirection — 301 permanent, 302 temporary), 4xx (client error — 400 bad request, 401 unauthorised, 403 forbidden, 404 not found), 5xx (server error — 500 internal server error, 503 service unavailable). HTTP/1.1 (1997): persistent connections (keep-alive — reuse TCP connections for multiple requests, avoiding handshake overhead for each); pipelining (send multiple requests without waiting for responses — but head-of-line blocking: a blocked response blocks all subsequent responses). HTTP/2 (2015): binary framing (machine-efficient vs. HTTP/1.1's text); multiplexing (multiple concurrent streams over a single TCP connection — eliminates HTTP-level head-of-line blocking); header compression (HPACK — dramatically reduces overhead of repeated headers); server push (server proactively sends resources it knows the client will need). HTTP/3 (2022): runs over QUIC instead of TCP. QUIC (UDP-based): eliminates TCP head-of-line blocking (one dropped packet no longer stalls unrelated streams); faster connection establishment (0-RTT or 1-RTT vs. TCP's 3-way handshake + TLS); built-in encryption (TLS 1.3 integrated into QUIC); connection migration (maintain connection when switching networks, identified by connection ID not IP). HTTPS: HTTP + TLS — encrypts the HTTP layer; TLS 1.3 is current standard."),

    ("What is a firewall and how do the different types work?",
     "A firewall is a network security device or software that monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predetermined security rules. It establishes a barrier between trusted internal networks and untrusted external networks. Types of firewalls: (1) Packet filter (first generation): inspects each packet independently based on source/destination IP, port, and protocol. Simple, fast, operates at Layer 3-4. Limitations: cannot detect attacks spread across multiple packets; no awareness of connection state. Implemented in routers using ACLs (access control lists). (2) Stateful packet inspection (second generation, now standard): tracks the state of active connections in a state table. Rules can be based on connection state (ESTABLISHED, RELATED, NEW). Can distinguish between legitimate response packets and unsolicited incoming packets. Much more secure than packet filters. iptables (Linux) and Windows Firewall are stateful. (3) Application layer firewall (WAF — Web Application Firewall): inspects payload content at Layer 7. Can detect and block application-specific attacks (SQL injection, XSS, CSRF) by parsing HTTP, DNS, FTP, etc. Requires deep packet inspection (DPI) — more resource intensive. Examples: ModSecurity, Cloudflare WAF, AWS WAF. (4) Next-generation firewall (NGFW): combines stateful inspection with application awareness (can identify applications regardless of port — e.g., BitTorrent), intrusion prevention (IPS — signature-based and anomaly detection), SSL inspection (decrypt, inspect, re-encrypt HTTPS), user identity awareness. Examples: Palo Alto, Fortinet, Cisco FTD. (5) Network address translation (NAT): translates private IP addresses (RFC 1918 — 192.168.x.x, 10.x.x.x) to a single public IP. Provides implicit firewall functionality (unsolicited inbound connections are blocked because there's no NAT mapping). Firewall placement: perimeter, DMZ (between external and internal), host-based (on individual endpoints)."),

    ("What is SSL/TLS and how does HTTPS protect web communications?",
     "TLS (Transport Layer Security) — successor to SSL (Secure Sockets Layer, now deprecated) — is the cryptographic protocol that provides authentication, integrity, and confidentiality for communications over a network. HTTPS = HTTP over TLS. TLS provides: (1) Authentication: server presents a digital certificate (X.509) signed by a trusted Certificate Authority (CA). The CA hierarchy: browser/OS vendors maintain a list of trusted root CAs; root CAs sign intermediate CAs; intermediate CAs sign server certificates. The browser verifies the certificate chain — ensuring the server is genuinely who it claims to be (prevents MITM attacks). Certificate contains: domain name, public key, validity period, CA signature. (2) Confidentiality: all data is encrypted — network observers see only encrypted bytes. (3) Integrity: MAC (message authentication code) ensures data has not been tampered with in transit. TLS 1.3 handshake (simplified): (1) ClientHello: client sends supported cipher suites, TLS version, random number, and key shares (for Diffie-Hellman). (2) ServerHello: server selects cipher suite, sends its key share, server random, and certificate. (3) Key derivation: both sides compute the session keys from their key shares (ECDHE — Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman Ephemeral). Session keys are never transmitted. (4) Server finished: server sends authentication data. (5) Client finished: secure channel established. 1-RTT (one round trip). 0-RTT resumption: clients that have previously connected can send data in the first message (replay attack risk — limited to idempotent requests). Cipher suite components: key exchange (ECDHE), authentication (RSA or ECDSA), bulk cipher (AES-256-GCM), MAC (HMAC-SHA384 or AEAD modes). Forward secrecy: use of ephemeral keys means compromise of the server's long-term private key does not expose past session data."),
]

NETWORKS = NETWORKS[:200]
write("s", NETWORKS)

